# Slide features ^features

!slide Two columns ^columns_demo

A slide can split into columns, and the step counter flows across them, so a
pause in one column defers what follows — including the next column.

!columns[60,40]
	!column
		The **left** column carries the main argument (60% of the width). It
		holds any lemur: lists, inline math like $\nabla \cdot \mathbf{E} =
		\rho/\varepsilon_0$, and its own pauses.

		- a static point, shown from the start
		- a second static point
		- a third, so this column is taller than the right one
		+ an incremental point (one keypress)

	!column[.center]
		!pause
		The **right** column (40%) is centered vertically, and waits for the
		left column's pause, so it appears only after the bullet on the left.

		!pause
		Its own pauses then work within the column.


!slide Emphasis on a keypress ^emphasis_demo

Parts of already-shown text can be colored or emphasized as you speak, without
moving anything on the slide (no arrows):

- convergence is the [interesting question]^q on loopy graphs
- the schedule is [surprisingly subtle]^sched
- and the whole iteration is [asynchronous by nature]^async

!annotate
	q[#c0392b .bold]:
	sched[.bold]:
	async[#2e5e6e .italic]:


!slide Overlay control ^overlays

Beyond `!pause`, you can say exactly __when__ a thing shows — and hide it
again, like LaTeX/beamer overlays. Step 0 is the slide as first shown; each
keypress advances the step (1, 2, 3, …), so `<1->` shows on the first keypress,
`<2>` only on the second, `<2-3>` during the second and third:

- shown from the start (step 0)
+<1-> appears on the first keypress, and stays
-<2> only on the second keypress — a transient aside
+<3-> appears on the third, and stays

!when<2-3>
	Everything indented under `!when` is gated together — here, visible on
	keypresses 2–3, then gone. `!when` blocks nest to narrow the window.


!slide Stacked overlays ^stacks

With `!stack`, layers overlap in one spot and **take the place of** each other
instead of stacking vertically. Each `!layer<spec>` says when it shows; the box
reserves the tallest layer, so nothing below it moves:

!stack
	!layer<0>
		- a variable node sends the product of its **incoming** messages
	!layer<1>
		- a factor node weighs by its **local factor** and marginalizes
	!layer<2->
		- on trees this is **exact**; on loopy graphs it iterates

… and the summary line stays put underneath as the point above swaps.


!slide Transitions ^transitions

`!transition <slide> [<step>]` sets two independent animations for the whole
deck. This talk uses **none** across slides; the `corporate` example deck uses
`!transition push rise` — see it there.

!columns[50,50]
	!column
		**Across slides** (first word):

		- `none` — cut, no animation
		- `fade` — cross-dissolve
		- `slide` — the whole slide slides
		- `push` — only the content slides; header/footer stay
	!column
		**Within a slide** (second word), as each `!pause`/`+`/overlay item
		appears:

		- `none` — appear instantly
		- `fade` — fade in
		- `rise` — lift up from below


!slide[.center .plain] One idea to take home

!gap[fill]
Message passing is **local computation plus a schedule** — and the schedule is
where the interesting engineering lives.
!gap[fill]

%% '.center' centers the content vertically; '.plain' drops the header/footer.
%% A theme (or base.css) styles '.slide.<name>', so the look is fully yours.
